<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.1.1: Contrasting Two Relationships by Reasoning</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00834</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.1.1: Contrasting Two Relationships by Reasoning</md:title>
    <md:uuid>52e969ab-8ec5-4ea2-be66-02a1b78f5cf3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up (15 minutes)</title>
<para>The goal of this opening activity is to activate, through a familiar context, what students know about functions from
  middle school.</para>
<para>Students first encounter a relationship in which two quantities—the number of bagels bought and price—do
  not form a function. They see that for some numbers of bagels bought, there are multiple possible prices. The
  quantities in the relationship between the number of bagels bought and the best price, however, do form a function
  because there is only one possible best price for each number of bagels.</para>
<para>Students contrast the two relationships by reasoning about possible prices, completing a table of values, and
  observing the graphs of the relationships.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<link url="https://teacher.desmos.com/activitybuilder/custom/645e928d9b6d13bb0bfc2de7">Desmos Activity: RAISE -
  Contrasting Two Relationships by Reasoning</link>
<para>Ask students to silently consider how each of the people mentioned in the scenario could be correct. You may wish for
  students to share their thinking with a shoulder partner and continue to work with that partner through the remainder
  of the activity.</para>
<para> Next, provide students with your Desmos class code so they may use it for the activity. In screen 1, students will
  be asked to create a table of the best price for the listed number of bagels. Next, in screens 2-4, students are
  provided with the table correctly filled out. Hannah's price is validated as the best price for 13 bagels, but
  students are asked to identify how the others could have arrived at their prices. As you monitor student conversations
  and responses, pause to display students' thinking. Be sure to highlight ideas that are different from others or have
  well worded responses. </para>
<para>In the last screen, students are asked to consider how a price list like the one in this scenario benefits a business
  owner and whether they like this type of pricing from a customer's point of view. Pause for a class discussion.</para>
<para>Desmos activities are different from the graphing calculator application because they lead students through one or
  more interactive tasks. As students progress through the activity, teachers may use the Teacher Dashboard to monitor
  student responses and use them for whole group discussion. Activities may also be set to identify student responses
  that are correct or incorrect. </para>
  <div class="os-raise-familysupport">
    <para>Access the <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e5b8beffe7473ad7fdafa2144d654c86bfe0e451">Desmos guide PDF</link> to learn more about accessing and customizing activities.</para>
</div>
<note class="extra-support">
  <title>Support for English Language Learners</title><para>MLR 8 Discussion Supports: Writing</para>
  <para>
  Encourage students to use the provided sentence stems to support their written explanations for how each person in
  the scenario might have determined their price for 13 bagels. The stems will also prove useful if you ask students to
  share their thinking verbally. 
    </para>
      <para><emphasis effect="italics">Design Principle: Optimize Output</emphasis></para></note>

<note class="extra-support">
  <title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title><para>Action and Expression: Develop Expression and Communication</para>
  <para> 
If needed, chunk the building of the table into stages. First, suggest students fill in values for the prices that
  are given. For instance, students would enter the cost for 1 bagel, 6 bagels, 9 bagels, and 12 bagels using the values
  from the price list. Next, prompt students to consider how much one bagel costs. Have them add that amount to each
  table entry that follows the given amounts. For instance, have them determine the costs to purchase 2-5 bagels. Then,
  determine the costs to buy 7 or 8 bagels, etc. </para>
      <para><emphasis effect="italics">Supports accessibility for: Conceptual processing; Attention</emphasis></para></note>

<para>Finally, to assist with determining how the other costs were obtained by the store worker, Jaime, and Pedro, ask
  students to identify pairs of numbers that sum to 13. For instance, 12 bagels + 1 bagel. Then match the price for each
  pair.</para>
  <title>Student Activity</title>
  <div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-align-items-center">
<figure><media alt="Trays of bagels at a bagel shop."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/af3ce9969e0d78e785c6b30de2d5ac1e50720b09"/></media></figure><figure><media alt="Fresh bagels sign. Prices listed. 1 bagel, 1 dollar 25 cents. 6 bagels, 6 dollars. 9 bagels, 8 dollars. 12 bagels, 10 dollars."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/80184470527f58641f0f1e23d4db7d7782de656d"/></media></figure></div>
    
  <para>A customer at a bagel shop is buying 13 bagels. The store worker says, “That would be $16.25.”
    Jaime, Pedro, and Hannah, who are in the shop, all think it is a mistake.</para>
  <list>
    <item>Jaime says, “Shouldn’t the total be $13.25?”</item>
    <item>Pedro says, “I think it should be $13.00.”</item>
    <item>Hannah says, “No, I think it should be $11.25.”</item>
  </list>
  <para>Consider how the store worker, Jaime, Pedro, and Hannah could all be right.
  </para>
<para>In the following activity, you will be asked to use the pricing information displayed at the bagel shop to determine
  who is correct about the pricing for 13 bagels. </para>
  <div class="os-raise-usermessage-link">
    <para>Log into <link url="https://student.desmos.com">student.desmos.com</link> using the information provided by your teacher to complete the activity.</para>
  </div>
